<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antiochus I Theos of Commagene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 70–38 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antiochus I Theos was king of Commagene from about 70 to 38 BCE. Claiming descent from both the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, he created a royal cult that merged Greek and Persian gods with himself, and built the great tomb sanctuary on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut">Mount Nemrut</a>. He ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> as a client of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which recognised him after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s eastern campaigns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antiochus-i-theos-of-commagene.jpg" alt="Antiochus I Theos of Commagene" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Verity Cridland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nemrut_Dag_area_A_Commagene_carving.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
